## 69. ASCII-Style Coding

Advanced questions may encode letters using character values.

For standard ASCII:

```text
A = 65
B = 66
...
Z = 90

a = 97
b = 98
...
z = 122
```

This is generally more relevant to programming-based puzzles than ordinary reasoning exams.

---

## 70. Binary Character Coding

A custom rule may convert alphabet position to binary.

Example:

```text
A = 1 = 00001
B = 2 = 00010
C = 3 = 00011
```

Therefore:

```text
CAT
C = 00011
A = 00001
T = 10100
```

Combined:

```text
000110000110100
```

---

## 71. Hexadecimal-Style Coding

A custom coding system might map:

```text
A=1
...
Z=26
```

then convert to hexadecimal.

Example:

```text
A = 1 = 01
B = 2 = 02
...
Z = 26 = 1A
```

This is advanced and uncommon in normal reasoning exams.

---

## 72. Matrix Coding

A matrix can assign coordinates to letters.

Example:

```text
      1  2  3  4  5
   +-----------------
1  | A  B  C  D  E
2  | F  G  H  I  J
3  | K  L  M  N  O
4  | P  Q  R  S  T
5  | U  V  W  X  Y
6  | Z
```

Then:

```text
A = 11
B = 12
C = 13
...
```

The first digit is row, second digit is column.

---

## 73. Polybius-Style Matrix

A common 5×5 form combines I/J:

```text
    1 2 3 4 5
  +-----------
1 | A B C D E
2 | F G H I K
3 | L M N O P
4 | Q R S T U
5 | V W X Y Z
```

Then:

```text
A = 11
B = 12
C = 13
...
```

This is useful for understanding matrix coding systems.

---

## 74. Matrix Coding Example

Using:

```text
A B C D E
F G H I K
L M N O P
Q R S T U
V W X Y Z
```

Encode:

```text
CAT
```

Coordinates:

```text
C = 13
A = 11
T = 44
```

Therefore:

```text
CAT → 13 11 44
```

---

## 75. Row-Column Vector Model

Represent each letter as:

```text
L = (row, column)
```

Example:

```text
C = (1,3)
T = (4,4)
```

So:

```text
CAT
=
(1,3)
(1,1)
(4,4)
```

This is a clean mathematical representation.

---

## 76. Vector Representation of Alphabet Coding

Represent alphabet letters as one-dimensional vectors:

```text
A = [1]
B = [2]
C = [3]
...
Z = [26]
```

A word becomes a vector:

```text
CAT = [3, 1, 20]
```

A coding transformation may be:

```text
Coded = Original + ShiftVector
```

Example:

```text
Original = [3,1,20]
Shift    = [1,2,3]

Coded    = [4,3,23]
```

Therefore:

```text
CAT → D C W
```

---

## 77. Transformation Vector

For:

```text
CAT → DBW
```

calculate differences:

```text
C → D = +1
A → B = +1
T → W = +3
```

So:

```text
Shift vector = [1,1,3]
```

If another word has the same length, apply:

```text
coded = original + [1,1,3]
```

---

## 78. Difference Table

A very useful solving technique:

| Position | Original | Code | Difference |
|---:|---|---|---:|
| 1 | C | D | +1 |
| 2 | A | B | +1 |
| 3 | T | W | +3 |

This immediately exposes many hidden patterns.

---
